  [PASS] 2a the correct prescription buys the satellite rows about 0.2 dex and no more.  h43/h44 used eq. 60, which is the algebraic force along the external-field axis and so the most suppressive member of the family; the flux theorem is the exact sphere average the virial estimator actually needs.  The ultra-faint offset falls by that amount and stays catastrophic.  This is the whole of what Angle C can deliver where the argument of nu was already right   (MW ultra-faints: eq.60 +1.650 -> flux theorem +1.438 dex (gain +0.212); isolated would be +0.709, so the EFE still supplies +0.729 dex of the failure)
  [PASS] 2b THE CONTROL THAT DOES NOT MOVE, and it is the reason no EFE prescription can be the answer.  The isolated Local Group field dwarfs carry NO external field, so every prescription gives them the same number by construction -- and they already sit within 0.1 dex of the relation at the same internal acceleration as the satellites that are 1.5 dex off it.  Whatever separates the two populations is not the EFE formula   (LG field dwarfs (N=13) sit at -0.088 dex identically under all five prescriptions, at y_int = 0.0297; the MW ultra-faints at y_int = 0.0008 sit at +1.438)

  2d THE nu-ARGUMENT ERROR APPEARS A SECOND TIME, in h42, and correcting it makes that row WORSE.  h42 computed
  the NGC 1052 group's external field as the MONDian one, g_ext = sqrt(G M_host a_0)/D = 0.15 a_0, and fed that
  to nu.  QUMOND wants the NEWTONIAN one, G M_host/D^2 = 0.023 a_0, which is 6.4x smaller and therefore
  suppresses far less.  Both are computed here:
  object         field used                    x_ext  sigma_pred  sigma_obs        B
  NGC1052-DF2    MONDian field, naive nu [h42]   0.1526        14.9        8.5   -0.487
  NGC1052-DF2    Newtonian field, naive nu    0.0233        22.5        8.5   -0.846
  NGC1052-DF2    Newtonian field, eq. 60      0.0233        14.9        8.5   -0.485
  NGC1052-DF2    Newtonian field, FLUX THEOREM   0.0233        19.7        8.5   -0.732
  NGC1052-DF4    MONDian field, naive nu [h42]   0.1526        16.6        4.2   -1.196
  NGC1052-DF4    Newtonian field, naive nu    0.0233        25.2        4.2   -1.555
  NGC1052-DF4    Newtonian field, eq. 60      0.0233        15.9        4.2   -1.155
  NGC1052-DF4    Newtonian field, FLUX THEOREM   0.0233        20.1        4.2   -1.362
  [PASS] 2d h42 CARRIES THE SAME ERROR AS h9 AND CORRECTING IT HURTS.  Using the QUMOND-correct Newtonian external field instead of the MONDian one weakens the external-field suppression by a factor 6 in its argument, raises the predicted dispersions from ~15 to ~20 km/s, and drives DF2 and DF4 FURTHER below the prediction.  The same correction that buys the Coma UDGs 0.25 dex costs these two 0.25 dex, for exactly the same reason and by exactly the same mechanism.  Note also what this settles: for a GALACTIC host the kernel-inverted and baryon-consistent fields coincide, because the framework does reproduce a galaxy's own dynamics from its baryons; they differ only for Coma, where it does not   (DF2 B: h42's recipe -0.487 -> corrected -0.732; DF4 -1.196 -> -1.362)

  2c THE ONE ROUTE LEFT OPEN, tested rather than assumed.  The satellites fail and the isolated field dwarfs do
  not, at the same internal acceleration.  If the field dwarfs were NOT really external-field-free -- if the
  large-scale structure supplied enough -- the EFE could still be the whole story.  Here they are given every
  external field the programme's other items measure, under the correct prescription:
  class                                  e_N=0    e_N=0.0046      e_N=0.01      e_N=0.03
  LG field dwarfs (EFE-FREE)            -0.088        -0.088        -0.086        -0.017
  LG field, gas-poor (EFE-FREE)         +0.118        +0.132        +0.216        +0.432
  [PASS] 2c THE ESCAPE IS CLOSED QUANTITATIVELY.  Giving the isolated field dwarfs the largest external field any item in this programme measures moves them by less than 0.1 dex, against the 1.5 dex that separates them from the ultra-faints.  They sit at HIGHER internal acceleration than the satellites, so the same external field does less to them -- the EFE cannot manufacture the population split even in principle   (field dwarfs move -0.088 -> -0.088 (e_N = 0.0046) -> -0.017 (e_N = 0.03), against a gap to the ultra-faints of 1.53 dex; closing it would need e_N far beyond anything measured and would then destroy the deep-tail a_0 (u03, check K1c))

