7.  MUTATION CONTROLS
==============================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M1 with the external field set to zero every prescription must give the identical number, or the differences reported above are not differences in the treatment of the external field   (max |difference| at g_ext = 0 is exactly 0.0e+00)
  [PASS] M2 raising the external field far above a_0 must collapse the whole family to a single function, because L(y) -> 0 there and every prescription becomes Newton.  If it did not, the family would have freedom the kernel does not give it   (span log10(perp/parallel) at y_ext = 10, 100, 1000: 0.0315, 0.0001, 0.0000 dex)
  [PASS] M3 shuffling which Milky Way satellite gets which external field must NOT reproduce the measured offset if the external field is carrying real information.  It very nearly does -- the shuffled distribution brackets the true value -- which is itself the finding: over this sample the external field varies too little from object to object for its assignment to matter, so the offset is a property of the SAMPLE, not of the EFE   (true median offset +1.109 dex; shuffled +1.219 +- 0.075 dex over 400 permutations (1.5 sigma))

==============================================================================================================================
SUMMARY
==============================================================================================================================
  1. The QUMOND external-field effect for a spherical system in a uniform external field has an EXACT closed
     form that this repository was not using: the sphere-averaged radial force equals the sphere average of the
     algebraic field (Gauss), which in the EFE-dominated limit is nu(y_e)(1 + L_e/3) G, not eq. 60's
     nu(y_e)(1 + L_e) G.  Verified against the field equation itself, monopole and quadrupole.
  2. Correcting it buys the SHORT rows about +0.20 dex and costs the LONG rows the same, because the EFE is a
     one-sided operator.  Coma UDGs +1.195 -> +1.083 (anchored field) or +0.942
     (baryonic field); MW ultra-faints +1.650 -> +1.438; tidal dwarfs and all four globulars get WORSE.
  3. Most of the Coma gain is not the prescription at all -- it is that h9 fed nu the cluster's TOTAL dynamical
     field instead of a Newtonian one.  The correction is real and it imports the standing cluster residual.
  4. NOT ONE of the four liabilities named in the brief is removed.  The whole admissible prescription span is
     0.32 dex and every one of them is larger than that.

  THE FOUR NAMED LIABILITIES, scored.  'best available' = the correct prescription with the most favourable
  external field that can be defended, which is not the same as the most favourable number in the table.
  liability                    as published   best available     gain   verdict
  Coma UDGs                          +1.195           +0.942   +0.253   REDUCED, not removed
  MW ultra-faint dwarfs              +1.650           +1.438   +0.212   REDUCED, not removed
  tidal dwarfs                       -0.394           -0.627   -0.233   WORSE
  binary galaxy pairs                +0.553           +0.553   +0.000   UNCHANGED
  (the binary pairs are unchanged by construction: h48's framework branch is already Milgrom's exact isolated
   deep-MOND two-body theorem, which contains no external-field prescription at all -- see u03 section 4, where
   every external-field branch is computed and every one of them fits worse.)

